/-!
Source-derived reading from Thomas Aquinas, *Summa theologiae* I q.4 a.2 co.
(*utrum in Deo sint perfectiones omnium rerum*), first proof.

Vocabulary as in the *quarta via* (`Question2.Article3QuartaVia`): `InAct c r`
says that `c` has the perfection `r`, formally or eminently, the same abstract
predicate as in the *prima via* and in q.11 a.3. `CausesIn c x esse` says that `c`
is a cause of `x`'s being, i.e. that `x` is an effect of `c`.

Source witness: SCTA diplomatic TEI of the Leonine edition, vol. 4 (Rome 1888),
`scta-texts/TAca84`, passage `AQUINAS-ST-I-Q4-A2`.
-/

namespace TruthCore.Latin.Aquinas.SummaTheologiae

universe u v

/-- Whatever perfection is in an effect must be found in its effective cause,
either in the same way (a univocal agent, as man begets man) or in a more
eminent way (an equivocal agent, as in the sun there is a likeness of what is
generated by its power) (*quidquid perfectionis est in effectu, oportet inveniri
in causa effectiva: vel secundum eandem rationem, si sit agens univocum ...; vel
eminentiori modo, si sit agens aequivocum*).

An effect is read as a thing whose being the cause causes, as in the
application that follows (*Cum ergo Deus sit prima causa effectiva rerum,
oportet omnium rerum perfectiones praeexistere in Deo*): every perfection of a
thing whose being `c` causes is in `c`, formally or eminently. The second proof
(from *ipsum esse subsistens*) is not formalized here.

Source: Thomas Aquinas, *Summa theologiae* I q.4 a.2 co., first proof.
-/
def PerfectionsOfEffectPreexistInCauseReading {Being : Sort u} {Respect : Sort v}
    (InAct : Being → Respect → Prop)
    (CausesIn : Being → Being → Respect → Prop) (esse : Respect) : Prop :=
  ∀ c x r, CausesIn c x esse → InAct x r → InAct c r

end TruthCore.Latin.Aquinas.SummaTheologiae
